# Badges B–C: checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.bugling
@complete complete
@note A bugle, trumpet or cornet may be used. Requirement 3 asks for a set number of calls from the official list.
@desc Bugling covers the history of the bugle, how brass instruments make sound, composing and sounding the traditional calls of camp life, caring for your instrument, and serving as your troop’s bugler.
1 k History of the bugle :: A brief history of the bugle and its use in camp and military life.
2 m Sound and composition :: How a bugle produces sound and how it relates to other brass instruments, and composing a call for your unit.
2a k How a bugle works :: How a bugle produces sound and how it relates to other brass instruments.
2b a Composing a call :: Composing a call for your troop or patrol and playing it for the unit.
3 demo Sounding calls :: Sounding the required number of calls from the official list.
4 k When calls are used :: When each of the calls from requirement 3 is used.
5 k Instrument care :: Cleaning, caring for and maintaining your instrument.
6 s Serving as bugler :: Serving as bugler for your troop for the required time.

@owner mb.canoeing
@complete partial
@note Excerpts showed the safety, equipment, handling, rescue and tandem-maneuver requirements but not every number, so only the confirmed opening requirement is outlined. Check the official list for numbering and distances.
@desc Canoeing teaches you to paddle safely and efficiently: hazards and first aid, the parts of a canoe and paddle, life jackets, carrying and launching, capsize recovery and rescues, and tandem and solo strokes and maneuvers.
@safety Paddling activities follow Safety Afloat and require a qualified supervisor; Scouts must pass the BSA swimmer test.
1 k! Hazards and first aid :: Hazards of canoeing and how to respond, and first aid for injuries such as blisters, hypothermia, heat reactions, sunburn and sprains.
= mb.swimming.1

@owner mb.chemistry
@complete partial
@note The official page showed eight requirements in its listing; the excerpts confirmed the topics of 1–7. Requirement 8 is not outlined yet.
@desc Chemistry is the science of what things are made of and how they change. The badge covers lab safety and safety data sheets, chemistry in the kitchen, experiments with water, fire and polymers, environmental chemistry, the fields of chemistry, the agencies that regulate chemicals, and careers.
@safety Experiments are done only under the supervision of a knowledgeable adult, with eye protection and the precautions on each product’s safety data sheet.
1 k! Chemistry and safety :: Safety data sheets, protective equipment, hazard pictograms and storing chemicals safely.
2 a Chemistry and cooking :: Kitchen chemistry experiments such as browning reactions and testing pH with a cabbage indicator.
3 a! Chemistry experiments :: Experiments such as waterproofing, flame tests and making a polymer, and the classes of fire.
4 k Chemistry and the environment :: How chemistry can reduce the environmental impact of products such as single-use items.
5 k Fields of chemistry :: Several fields of chemistry and how they interact.
6 k Regulating chemicals :: Government agencies that oversee chemicals and their responsibilities.
7 a Chemistry careers :: One option: visiting a lab or plant, or researching chemistry careers.

@owner mb.chess
@note Chess was reorganized for 2026 and now includes researching a famous chess player, how tournaments are run, and solving mate problems. The excerpts were too jumbled to outline the numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Chess covers the history of the game, notation, how the pieces move, basic strategy and tactics, famous players, how tournaments are run, solving mate problems, and teaching or organizing play.

@owner mb.climbing
@complete partial
@note The official page lists the topics in order, but the excerpts did not show clean numbering for the belaying, climbing and rappelling requirements, so those are not outlined yet.
@desc Climbing teaches safe climbing, bouldering and rappelling: hazards and first aid, gear and grading, site safety, verbal signals, rope care and the essential knots, and climbing and rappelling under a qualified instructor.
@safety Climbing and rappelling follow Climb On Safely and require a qualified instructor.
1 k! Hazards and first aid :: Hazards of climbing and first aid for climbing injuries.
3 k Climbing gear and grades :: Clothing and gear, how climbs are graded, and top-roping, lead climbing and bouldering.
4 k! Site safety :: Checking a climbing site and keeping the area safe.
5 demo Verbal signals :: Signals for climbers, rappellers, belayers and boulderers.
6 demo Rope care :: Inspecting, caring for, retiring and coiling a climbing rope.
7 demo Climbing knots :: The knots used in climbing, such as the figure eight follow-through.
8 demo Harness and helmet :: Fitting a harness and helmet and the gear you wear.

@owner mb.coin-collecting
@complete partial
@note Excerpts confirmed requirements 1 and 4–7; requirements 2 and 3 were not visible and are not outlined yet.
@desc Coin Collecting introduces numismatics: how coins are made, grading and caring for coins, the designs and designers of U.S. coins and paper money, commemorative issues, and building a collection of world coins, notes or tokens.
1 k How coins are made :: How coins are made and where the U.S. Mint facilities are.
4 a U.S. coin designs :: Collecting U.S. coins of each denomination and explaining their designs, designers and special programs.
5 k Bicentennial designs :: The special designs struck for the U.S. Bicentennial.
6 k Paper money :: The people on U.S. paper currency and where it is printed.
7 a A collection or experience :: One option: a collection of world coins, notes or tokens, or a coin show, club meeting or mint visit.
